/ActualText (Why attitude is formed?)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
330 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1147 0 R
/ActualText (Utilitarian Function: Utilitarian function is related to the basic principles of reward and punishment. We develop some attitudes toward products simply on the basis of whether these products provide pleasure or pain. If a person likes the taste of a cheeseburger, that person will develop a positive attitude toward cheeseburgers. Ads that stress straightforward product benefits \(e.g., you should drink Diet Coke "just for the taste of it"\) appeal to the utilitarian function.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
331 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1148 0 R
/ActualText (Value-Expressive Function: Value-expressive attitudes reflect a consumer's core values or self-concept, influencing product choices not for their objective benefits but for what they signify about the person \(e.g., 'What sort of man reads Playboy?'\). These attitudes are crucial in lifestyle analyses, as they help marketers understand how consumers use products to express their identity and values, guiding strategies that align brands with specific consumer lifestyles.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
332 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1149 0 R
/ActualText (Ego-Defensive Function: Attitudes that are formed to protect the person, either from external threats or internal feelings of insecurity, perform an ego-defensive function. An early marketing study indicated that housewives in the 1950s resisted the use of instant coffee because it threatened their conception of themselves as capable homemakers. Products that promise to help a man project a "macho" image \(e.g., Marlboro cigarettes\) may be appealing to his insecurities about his masculinity. Many deodorant campaigns stress the dire, embarrassing consequences of being caught with underarm odor in public.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
