/Type /StructElem
/S /H5
/T (2. Variability in Services Services are subject to variability because they are provided by people, and the quality can differ depending on who delivers the service and when. To minimize this variability, companies implement standardized processes. For example, a lecture might vary depending on whether it's given by Professor A or Professor B, but standardized curricula help ensure consistency. Similarly, chain restaurants use specific recipes and training to ensure the same dish tastes consistent regardless of the chef or time of day.)
/E (2. Variability in Services Services are subject to variability because they are provided by people, and the quality can differ depending on who delivers the service and when. To minimize this variability, companies implement standardized processes. For example, a lecture might vary depending on whether it's given by Professor A or Professor B, but standardized curricula help ensure consistency. Similarly, chain restaurants use specific recipes and training to ensure the same dish tastes consistent regardless of the chef or time of day.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [41]
>>
endobj
527 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H5
/T (3. Simultaneity in Services Services are simultaneously produced and consumed, often requiring the provider and the consumer to be present at the same time. Through proper training, service providers can reduce variability and enhance consistency in service delivery. For example, in a salon, barbers are trained to cut hair efficiently, ensuring that each customer receives a consistent and high-quality experience. Similarly, in a live concert, performers are trained to engage with the audience while delivering a consistent performance, ensuring that all attendees receive the same level of service simultaneously. Training equips service providers to handle the challenges of simultaneity.)
/E (3. Simultaneity in Services Services are simultaneously produced and consumed, often requiring the provider and the consumer to be present at the same time. Through proper training, service providers can reduce variability and enhance consistency in service delivery. For example, in a salon, barbers are trained to cut hair efficiently, ensuring that each customer receives a consistent and high-quality experience. Similarly, in a live concert, performers are trained to engage with the audience while delivering a consistent performance, ensuring that all attendees receive the same level of service simultaneously. Training equips service providers to handle the challenges of simultaneity.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [42]
>>
endobj
528 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H5
/T (4. Perishability in Services Services are perishable, meaning they cannot be stored for future use; once the service time passes, it cannot be recovered. To manage this, companies often use dynamic pricing strategies to maximize utilization. For example, airline seats are perishable; if they are not filled by the time a flight departs, the opportunity for revenue is lost. Airlines address this by offering lower prices months in advance and adjusting prices closer to departure based on demand. Similarly, hotel rooms are perishable, so hotels adjust prices based on seasonality, offering discounts in low seasons and raising rates during peak times.)
/E (4. Perishability in Services Services are perishable, meaning they cannot be stored for future use; once the service time passes, it cannot be recovered. To manage this, companies often use dynamic pricing strategies to maximize utilization. For example, airline seats are perishable; if they are not filled by the time a flight departs, the opportunity for revenue is lost. Airlines address this by offering lower prices months in advance and adjusting prices closer to departure based on demand. Similarly, hotel rooms are perishable, so hotels adjust prices based on seasonality, offering discounts in low seasons and raising rates during peak times.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [43]
>>
