754 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Perceived value focuses on how customers view the value of a product, rather than just its price. The key idea is that the true cost of a product includes not only the purchase price but also other associated costs, like maintenance or replacement.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
755 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (Example: Imagine two cutting tools: Tool A costs $1 and lasts for 30 hours of use. However, every 30 hours, it needs to be replaced, which takes 20 minutes. The average labor cost in the U.S. is $15 per hour, so the labor cost to replace Tool A every time is $5 \(20 minutes is 1/3rd of an hour, so $15/3 = $5\). Over 120 hours of work, you would need to replace Tool A four times, resulting in a total cost of $19 \(4 replacements at $1 each plus 4 labor costs of $5 each\))
/E (Example: Imagine two cutting tools: Tool A costs $1 and lasts for 30 hours of use. However, every 30 hours, it needs to be replaced, which takes 20 minutes. The average labor cost in the U.S. is $15 per hour, so the labor cost to replace Tool A every time is $5 \(20 minutes is 1/3rd of an hour, so $15/3 = $5\). Over 120 hours of work, you would need to replace Tool A four times, resulting in a total cost of $19 \(4 replacements at $1 each plus 4 labor costs of $5 each\))
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
756 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (Tool B costs $5 but lasts for 120 hours without needing replacement. The only labor involved is the initial setup, which takes 20 minutes, costing $5 in labor. The total cost of Tool B for 120 hours of work is therefore $5 \(the cost of the tool plus the labor for setup\). Even though Tool A has a lower upfront cost, its total cost over 120 hours of work is much higher than Tool B's. This demonstrates that Tool B provides greater value due to fewer replacements and lower overall costs, even if its initial price is higher.)
/E (Tool B costs $5 but lasts for 120 hours without needing replacement. The only labor involved is the initial setup, which takes 20 minutes, costing $5 in labor. The total cost of Tool B for 120 hours of work is therefore $5 \(the cost of the tool plus the labor for setup\). Even though Tool A has a lower upfront cost, its total cost over 120 hours of work is much higher than Tool B's. This demonstrates that Tool B provides greater value due to fewer replacements and lower overall costs, even if its initial price is higher.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
